**New evidence**
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"G.R. No. 260374","publisher":"LawPhil / Supreme Court of the Philippines","publication_date":"2022-06-07","url":"https://lawphil.net/judjuris/juri2022/jun2022/gr_260374_2022.html","claim":"The 2022 Supreme Court opinion recounts Marcos Jr.'s 1997 conviction for failing to file income tax returns for 1982-1985 and notes the Comelec's 2022 finding that the record did not show a voluntary, intentional violation or moral turpitude.","locator":"Decision text and the section summarizing the Comelec resolutions in the 2022 petition proceedings","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official court decision; the only limitation is that the page is difficult to render cleanly, so I relied on the decision text surfaced in the search result.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly litigated in 2022, with underlying conduct reaching back to the 1980s and staying salient in the 2022 candidacy challenge.","ruler_attribution":"Directly about Marcos Jr.'s own tax conviction and the legal position advanced in his name; strong personal nexus.","contrary_evidence":["Comelec and the Court treated the conviction as non-disqualifying in that proceeding.","The decision does not itself prove current-office self-dealing."],"lenses":["7B.2","7B.6","7B.8"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"QC court certifies Bongbong Marcos didn’t pay tax dues, fines","publisher":"Philippine Daily Inquirer","publication_date":"2021-12-04","url":"https://newsinfo.inquirer.net/1523524/qc-court-certifies-marcos-jr-didnt-pay-tax-dues-fines","claim":"A Quezon City RTC officer certified there was no record showing Marcos Jr. had satisfied the court's order to settle tax deficiencies and pay the fine after his tax-evasion conviction; the article also says his spokesperson claimed compliance but would not provide the payment details.","locator":"Lines 119-123 and 140-168 in the article text","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct report of a named court certification with explicit quotations from the spokesperson and petitioners' lawyers.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Pre-2022 background that remained directly relevant to the 2022 integrity question because the tax-compliance issue was still unresolved as he entered office.","ruler_attribution":"Directly about Marcos Jr.'s own tax obligations and his spokesperson's response; strong personal nexus.","contrary_evidence":["Marcos's spokesperson asserted compliance.","The article is reporting a certification, not a new adjudication of guilt."],"lenses":["7B.2","7B.3","7B.4","7B.8","7B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Trolls? ‘Show me one, they don’t exist’ – Marcos Jr.","publisher":"Philippine Daily Inquirer","publication_date":"2022-04-27","url":"https://newsinfo.inquirer.net/1588374/marcos-camp-has-online-trolls-show-me-one-they-dont-exist-says-bongbong","claim":"In a CNN Philippines interview, Marcos Jr. denied using troll farms, saying he had been offered a click army but did not use it and insisting there were no trolls.","locator":"Quoted passage in the article body","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct quotation from Marcos Jr. in a contemporary interview, reported by a major outlet.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"April 2022 campaign period, squarely within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal statement by Marcos Jr.","contrary_evidence":["Later fact-checking and other reporting contradict the denial.","The statement alone does not prove authorship of every online account."],"lenses":["7B.1","7B.2","7B.8","7B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Marcos’ ‘confessions’: Philippine president-elect admits to ‘trolls’, needing guidance – and doing it for his parents","publisher":"South China Morning Post","publication_date":"2022-06-14","url":"https://www.scmp.com/week-asia/politics/article/3181416/marcos-confessions-philippines-president-elect-admits-trolls","claim":"SCMP reports a post-election video in which Marcos Jr. appeared to acknowledge use of a troll army to sustain political fortunes and said he did it for his parents / for guidance.","locator":"Article lede and first paragraphs summarizing the video","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Secondary reporting on a video; the page itself was blocked, so the claim is taken from the accessible search-result text and should be treated cautiously.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"June 2022, immediately after the election and during the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributed to Marcos Jr. as reported from his own recorded remarks, but the evidentiary chain is secondhand.","contrary_evidence":["The reporting is not the original video transcript.","The context is a celebratory post-election setting, so phrasing may be informal or partial."],"lenses":["7B.1","7B.2","7B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Hindi raw involved si Ferdinand Marcos Jr. sa mga troll farm?","publisher":"Tsek.ph / eBoto","publication_date":"2022-04-28","url":"https://www.tsek.ph/hindi-raw-involved-si-ferdinand-marcos-jr-sa-mga-troll-farm/","claim":"The fact-check says Marcos Jr.'s denial of troll farms is false, citing studies, investigations, and testimony; it also notes more than 300 Marcos-linked Twitter accounts were suspended in January 2022 for platform manipulation and spam.","locator":"Lines 63-70 of the fact-check page","source_type":"NGO/fact-check","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Election fact-checking project with explicit reasoning and named supporting facts; strong corroborative value even though it is not a court finding.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Campaign-year 2022 evidence directly on point.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Marcos Jr.'s public denial and to accounts linked to his campaign ecosystem.","contrary_evidence":["The fact-check is not a judicial finding.","Some support is inferential through linked accounts and investigations rather than a single direct confession."],"lenses":["7B.1","7B.2","7B.7","7B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Philippines","publisher":"Reuters Institute for the Study of Journalism, University of Oxford","publication_date":"2022-06-15","url":"https://reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk/digital-news-report/2022/philippines","claim":"The country report says Marcos held professional journalists in low regard during the campaign, barred them from campaign events, snubbed media-hosted debates, and favored friendly influencers and a partisan TV station; it also notes vilification of journalists and fact-checkers and DDoS attacks on newsrooms.","locator":"Lines 32-40 of the country report","source_type":"scholarship/report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reputable annual research report with specific, time-bound observations and multiple corroborating examples.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Mid-2022 campaign-period evidence, directly within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct campaign behavior by Marcos Jr. is reported alongside attacks by third parties; attribution is strongest for the campaign access choices and media treatment.","contrary_evidence":["Some of the abuse described was carried out by third parties, not Marcos personally.","The report also shows an active fact-checking and media response ecosystem."],"lenses":["7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Online posts misrepresent reports on estate tax liability of Marcoses","publisher":"Tsek.ph / Fact Check Patrol","publication_date":"2022-04-30","url":"https://www.tsek.ph/online-posts-misrepresent-reports-on-estate-tax-liability-of-marcoses/","claim":"The fact-check says online posts misrepresented reports as if the Marcoses had already settled estate-tax liabilities; officials said the government was still pursuing collection and the liabilities were believed to have ballooned from P23 billion to P203 billion.","locator":"Lines 65-76 of the fact-check page","source_type":"NGO/fact-check","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Clear fact-check with named officials and quantified liabilities; useful for separating rumor from the underlying unresolved tax issue.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"April 2022, directly in the target year and election period.","ruler_attribution":"The tax liability is family-linked rather than a discrete act by Marcos Jr., so the personal nexus is inferential but still salient for family-interest analysis.","contrary_evidence":["The piece focuses on supporters' online posts rather than Marcos Jr.'s own direct statement.","It concerns the Marcos estate, not a personal tax filing by Bongbong Marcos in 2022."],"lenses":["7B.3","7B.4","7B.7","7B.8","7B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Marcos return to Malacañang funded by donors linked to father’s ‘cronies’, gov’t contractors","publisher":"Philippine Center for Investigative Journalism","publication_date":"2022-08-31","url":"https://pcij.org/2022/08/31/marcos-return-to-malacaang-funded-by-donors-linked-to-fathers-cronies-govt-contractors/","claim":"PCIJ found that Marcos Jr.'s successful 2022 campaign was funded by kin of his father's supporters and by business interests, including owners of firms barred by law from political contributions; it says 67 donors contributed P624.7 million and that Anton Lagdameo's party donation was later followed by his appointment as Special Assistant to the President.","locator":"Lines 81-87, 104-107, 120-123, and 180-185 in the article text","source_type":"NGO/investigative","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Deep investigative report grounded in Comelec declarations and public records, with explicit discussion of legal loopholes and post-election appointments.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Fully within the 2022 campaign and transition period.","ruler_attribution":"Direct campaign-finance nexus to Marcos Jr. and his immediate governing circle; some donor conduct is indirect but the appointment link is specific.","contrary_evidence":["The article notes contributions were routed through individuals, exploiting a legal loophole.","It does not itself prove quid pro quo or illegal influence."],"lenses":["7B.3","7B.4","7B.5","7B.9","7B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bongbong Marcos Jr. picks ex-solon Anton Lagdameo as Special Assistant to the President","publisher":"Philippine Daily Inquirer","publication_date":"2022-05-26","url":"https://newsinfo.inquirer.net/1602831/marcos-jr-picks-ex-solon-anton-lagdameo-as-special-assistant-to-the-president","claim":"Marcos said he chose Anton Lagdameo as Special Assistant to the President because they had worked together for the past decade and Lagdameo knew him very well.","locator":"Quoted remarks in the article body and headline","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous reporting of Marcos's own stated rationale for a sensitive appointment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Pre-inauguration 2022 transition period.","ruler_attribution":"Direct appointment choice by Marcos Jr.","contrary_evidence":["The justification is familiarity and trust, not merit alone.","The appointee was also linked by PCIJ to campaign finance and donor networks."],"lenses":["7B.5","7B.9","7B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Cabinet members, economic team joining the Marcos administration","publisher":"BusinessWorld","publication_date":"2022-06-30","url":"https://www.bworldonline.com/special-features/2022/06/30/458451/cabinet-members-economic-team-joining-the-marcos-administration/","claim":"The article lists the incoming cabinet and notes that Marcos selected several experienced professionals for key roles, including Diokno, Balisacan, Medalla, Pascual, Pangandaman, Bonoan, and others; it also notes campaign manager Benhur Abalos, Sara Duterte, Enrile, and Tulfos-linked appointments that drew political-link criticism.","locator":"Lines 99-137 of the article text","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting with a concrete roster of appointees and their prior experience, plus explicit notes on politically connected choices.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Immediate transition and first day of office in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct appointment choices by Marcos Jr.; mixed because it contains both professional and political selections.","contrary_evidence":["Several appointees were political allies or campaign figures, not technocrats.","A positive reading of competence does not by itself rule out favoritism."],"lenses":["7B.5","7B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bongbong Marcos party declares P272-M campaign spending in Eleksyon 2022","publisher":"GMA News Online","publication_date":"2022-06-06","url":"https://www.gmanetwork.com/news/topstories/nation/834032/marcos-party-declares-p272-m-campaign-spending-in-eleksyon-2022/story/","claim":"PFP reported P272 million in campaign spending, filed a 400-page SOCE, and said the document was signed by Anton Lagdameo, who Marcos had already chosen as Special Assistant to the President.","locator":"Article body describing the SOCE filing and Lagdameo's role","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"A contemporaneous report of the party's official disclosure filing, with identifiable figures and a public record trail.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Election-period 2022 evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly linked to Marcos's party and his incoming chief aide; the disclosure itself is official, though the article is secondary reporting.","contrary_evidence":["The article frames the amount as below the legal spending ceiling.","Official disclosure does not establish donor purity or absence of influence."],"lenses":["7B.6","7B.8","7B.9","7B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Executive Order No. 1, s. 2022","publisher":"LawPhil / Malacañang","publication_date":"2022-06-30","url":"https://lawphil.net/executive/execord/eo2022/eo_1_2022.html","claim":"Marcos's first executive order abolished the Presidential Anti-Corruption Commission and transferred its powers to the Office of the Deputy Executive Secretary for Legal Affairs, citing streamlining and avoidance of duplicative functions.","locator":"Section 1 of Executive Order No. 1, s. 2022","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official presidential order; the only limitation is that the page itself was hard to render, so the search-result text was used as the quote source.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exactly the first day of the Marcos presidency in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct presidential act by Marcos Jr.","contrary_evidence":["The EO gives an efficiency rationale rather than an explicit anti-accountability rationale.","It abolishes PACC, not the PCGG that handles Marcos-family wealth cases."],"lenses":["7B.7","7B.9"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Gov’t told not to abolish PCGG during Marcos rule","publisher":"BusinessWorld","publication_date":"2022-08-28","url":"https://www.bworldonline.com/the-nation/2022/08/28/471054/govt-told-not-to-abolish-pcgg-during-marcos-rule/","claim":"Analysts warned that abolishing or weakening the PCGG during Marcos Jr.'s rule would pose a conflict of interest because the agency exists to recover the Marcos family's ill-gotten wealth and unresolved cases were still pending.","locator":"Article body and analyst quotations in the opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous coverage quoting named analysts and the PCGG chair on an obvious conflict-of-interest concern.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022, after Marcos assumed office.","ruler_attribution":"Not a finding of wrongdoing, but the conflict-of-interest warning is explicitly tied to his presidency and his family name.","contrary_evidence":["The article reports this as analyst opinion rather than adjudicated fact.","Supporters of abolition argued the PCGG had outlived its usefulness."],"lenses":["7B.4","7B.7","7B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"After earlier hesitation, Marcos says 'perfectly willing to release' SALNs","publisher":"Philstar.com","publication_date":"2022-01-24","url":"https://www.philstar.com/headlines/2022/01/24/2156057/after-earlier-hesitation-marcos-says-perfectly-willing-release-salns","claim":"Marcos said he was 'perfectly willing' to release his SALNs but also said he would rather not release them if the disclosures would be used for politics and suggested authorities investigate questionable wealth instead.","locator":"Quoted paragraph in the article body and headline","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous quote, but it is conditional and paired with an express hesitation, so it is better treated as mixed transparency evidence than as full disclosure.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Campaign-period 2022 evidence directly preceding his presidency.","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal statement by Marcos Jr.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is conditional and not a clean release commitment.","It suggests he preferred channeling scrutiny through authorities rather than public disclosure."],"lenses":["7B.6","7B.8","7B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across LawPhil, the Supreme Court E-Library, Comelec, PCIJ, Reuters Institute, AFP fact checks, Tsek.ph, Inquirer, BusinessWorld, GMA, Philstar, ABS-CBN, and SCMP.

I kept 13 new source-claim units plus 5 reused items, with emphasis on items that showed a direct personal nexus: Marcos's own tax-compliance record, his own public denial and apparent later admission around troll farms, his campaign-finance network, his appointment of campaign-linked officials, and his first-day executive order dismantling an anti-corruption body.

I rejected or downgraded:
- 2023-2025 follow-ups that were outside the target year unless they clearly served as retrospective evidence about a 2022 act.
- Broad misinformation or press-freedom pieces that did not tie tightly enough to Marcos Jr.'s own statements, appointments, or use of office.
- Duplicate syndications of the same story.
- Articles that mainly described the Marcos family, the broader political environment, or inherited corruption without a usable ruler-specific nexus.
- Items that were too general to separate personal integrity from ordinary political propaganda.

Blocked or technically awkward pages:
- LawPhil and some Inquirer/SCMP pages were not cleanly openable in-browser, so I relied on the accessible search-result text or alternate accessible mirrors when the claim was independently corroborated.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct documentary evidence on Marcos Jr.'s own SALN content, asset changes, or 2022 personal financial handling is still thin.
- The strongest negative items are a mix of his own statements, campaign-network evidence, and family-linked financial issues; fewer items show direct personal self-enrichment in office itself.
- More search could still help on 7B.3 and 7B.4 by finding primary records on campaign finance, asset disclosure, or a court/ombudsman record tied directly to his own conduct rather than the broader Marcos family.
